.TH tftpd 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tftpd \- a trivial file transfer protocol server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tftpd
[\fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR]
[\fIdirectory\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tftpd
serves files over TFTP (RFC 1350) from
.I directory
if one is given, or from the directory it was started in, one transfer
at a time.
.PP
TFTP is the protocol every router, workstation and build script already
has a client for, so this is the shortest way to move a file on or off
the machine without a card reader.  Both directions work: a read takes
a copy away, a write creates the file here, replacing one of the same
name.
.PP
A file is opened relative to the serving directory.  A leading
.B /
is dropped and a path containing
.B ..
is refused: there is no chroot here and the whole disc is one
namespace.
.TP
.B \-p
Serve on
.I port
instead of 69.  Port 69 is privileged and needs root.
.TP
.B \-q
Say nothing.  Without it each request and each completion is reported.
.TP
.B \-r
Refuse writes.  Reads still work.
.SH EXAMPLES
Take a copy of something on the board, from another machine:
.nf

# mkdir /tmp/pub
# cp hello.bas /tmp/pub
# tftpd /tmp/pub &
1234

  (elsewhere)
  curl -o hello.bas tftp://192.168.1.245/hello.bas
  curl -T new.bas tftp://192.168.1.245/new.bas
.fi
.SH STOPPING IT
Note the process id the shell prints when you background it, and
.B kill
that.  The shell has no job control, so
.B kill %1
does not work here; if you have lost the number,
.B ps
will find it.
.PP
Stop it before flashing or rebooting.  A running server holds the
filesystem open, so
.B remount \-n / ro
refuses and the card goes down dirty.
.SH NOTES
Transfers are 512 bytes a block, acknowledged one at a time, so TFTP is
never fast.  The options of RFC 2347 \- blksize, tsize, timeout \- are
ignored, which is what that RFC asks of a server that does not
implement them: the client sees no reply to them and falls back.
.PP
netascii is not converted.  Both modes move the bytes through
unchanged, and the mode is only reported, so use octet \- which is what
every client uses by default for anything that is not text.
.PP
A block that is not acknowledged is sent again after ten seconds, five
times, and the transfer is then abandoned.
.PP
There is no authentication in TFTP at all, and a write replaces any
file of that name in the serving directory.  Serve a directory you are
willing to have written to, or use
.BR \-r .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR httpd (1),
.BR htget (1),
.BR wifi (1),
.BR remount (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR ps (1)
